= Solution
{parent=i}

Any isomorphism would permute <irreducible components> and preserve their intersections. In each configuration, the origin is the unique point lying on all three components, so an isomorphism must take origin to origin.

The <coordinate ring> of the spatial configuration is
$$
A=k[x,y,z]/(xy,xz,yz).
$$
The displayed ideal is indeed the intersection of the three coordinate-axis ideals. Its generators have no linear parts, so at the origin the residue classes of $x,y,z$ form a basis of $\mathfrak m/\mathfrak m^2$. Hence its <algebraic cotangent space> and <Zariski tangent space> have dimension three.

For the plane configuration, the <coordinate ring> is $B=k[s,t]/(st(s-t))$. The three linear factors are distinct in every characteristic, so this principal ideal is radical. It likewise has no linear part, but now $\mathfrak n/\mathfrak n^2$ has basis $s,t$ and dimension two. An isomorphism of <local rings> induces an isomorphism of these cotangent spaces, which is impossible:
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{edim}\mathcal O_{X,0}=3\ne2=\operatorname{edim}\mathcal O_{Y,0}.}
$$
Thus **the varieties are not isomorphic**. This is <embedding dimension distinguishes three-branch curves>: matching incidence of branches does not determine their local algebra.

\Image[paper-21-three-branches.png]
{title=Three spatial axes and three coplanar lines have different tangent-space dimensions at their common point}

= ii
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Parametrize the line as $\ell(t)=x+t(x'-x)$ and set $F(t)=f(\ell(t))$, a polynomial of degree at most three. The <Jacobian criterion> at a singular point of a hypersurface gives vanishing of its equation and all first derivatives. Consequently
$$
F(0)=F(1)=0,\qquad
F'(0)=\sum_j(x'_j-x_j)f_{t_j}(x)=0,\qquad
F'(1)=\sum_j(x'_j-x_j)f_{t_j}(x')=0.
$$
Thus $t^2$ and $(t-1)^2$ both divide $F$. They are coprime in every characteristic, so their degree-four product divides a polynomial of degree at most three. It follows that $F=0$, proving
$$
\boxed{\ell(\mathbb A^1)\subseteq X.}
$$
This is the <secant line through singular points of a cubic hypersurface> argument. If the given defining polynomial has repeated factors, use its square-free part for the reduced variety's Jacobian criterion: vanishing of that polynomial and its gradient at a singular point implies vanishing of the original polynomial's gradient there as well, by the product rule. The same restriction argument therefore still applies.

